Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Stylized period storybook animation with softly textured, hand-painted characters and environments.

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Firelight warms a turn-of-the-century nursery. A fat, bunchy VELVETEEN RABBIT sits atop a stocking, his coat spotted brown and white, his ears lined with pink sateen, and holly tucked between his thread whiskers.

(SOUND: a fire crackles; distant church bells ring through the frosted windows)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

THE BOY, six, with a round face and chestnut bowl-cut hair, bounds in wearing a white nightshirt. He pulls the VELVETEEN RABBIT from the stocking, hugs him, and kisses his pink nose.

Parcels arrive in a great rustle. The nursery fills with ribbons, tissue paper, a toy engine, and a clockwork mouse. THE BOY races from one present to another.

(SOUND: paper tears, a clockwork motor buzzes, wooden train wheels clatter)

The VELVETEEN RABBIT slips unnoticed behind the mound of discarded paper.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For at least two hours the Boy loved him. Then, in the excitement of looking at all the new presents, the Velveteen Rabbit was forgotten.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Weeks later, the VELVETEEN RABBIT lies on the nursery floor. His brown-and-white coat remains soft, but dust dulls his boot-button eyes.

(SOUND: a clockwork mouse whirs past; the nursery clock ticks steadily)

Mechanical toys parade and swagger. A model boat displays its rigging while a jointed wooden lion poses beside the toy cupboard. The shy VELVETEEN RABBIT shrinks against the fender.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The mechanical toys were very superior. They were full of modern ideas and pretended they were real.

Beside the fender rests SKIN HORSE, an old brown toy horse with bald patches, visible seams, patient glass eyes, and only a few hairs left in his tail.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

The VELVETEEN RABBIT looks uneasily at SKIN HORSE'S bare seams.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time.

SKIN HORSE lowers his worn head beside the Rabbit.

SKIN HORSE
Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out, and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are Real?

SKIN HORSE smiles.

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago. But once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

(MUSIC: a tender music-box melody rises beneath the ticking clock)

The VELVETEEN RABBIT touches his pink nose and glossy whiskers, longing and apprehension mingling in his button eyes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He longed to become Real, and yet the idea of growing shabby and losing his eyes and whiskers was rather sad.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

A night-light burns on the mantelpiece. THE BOY waits beneath a patchwork quilt while NANA, a stout middle-aged woman with an iron-gray bun, black dress, and white apron, searches the room.

(SOUND: drawers open and close; bedsprings creak; the mantel clock chimes eight)

Unable to find the china dog, NANA swoops upon the open toy cupboard and drags the VELVETEEN RABBIT out by one ear.

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She puts the Rabbit into THE BOY'S waiting arms.

A procession of nights passes. THE BOY hugs the Rabbit tight, rolls over him, and pushes him beneath the pillow. Soon he builds tunnels under the bedclothes and guides the Rabbit through them.

(SOUND: quilts rustle, tiny whispered games fade into a sleepy sigh)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Very soon he grew to like it. When the Boy dropped off to sleep, the Rabbit snuggled close under his warm chin, with the Boy's hands clasped round him all night long.

More nights pass. Kisses rub the pink from the Rabbit's nose. His tail loosens, his velveteen coat grows thin, and one thread whisker after another disappears.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The little Rabbit was so happy that he never noticed how his beautiful velveteen fur was getting shabbier and shabbier.

EXT. GARDEN - SPRING EVENING

Spring gold fills the walled garden. THE BOY, chestnut-haired and round-faced, pushes the VELVETEEN RABBIT in a green wheelbarrow; the Rabbit's spotted coat is now rubbed thin and his pink nose has faded.

(SOUND: wheelbarrow wheels squeak, bees hum, and THE BOY laughs breathlessly)

THE BOY gives the Rabbit a picnic on the grass, digs burrows in the flower bed, and builds him a fairy hut beneath the raspberry canes.

NANA rings a handbell from the house. THE BOY drops his little spade and runs inside, leaving the VELVETEEN RABBIT on the lawn.

Sunset drains from the garden. Dew beads across the Rabbit's threadbare coat while moths flutter above him.

(SOUND: crickets replace the daytime birds; the distant handbell rings again)

NANA returns with her iron-gray hair tightly pinned, her white apron bright in the dusk, and a candle cupped in one hand. She finds the Rabbit wet, earthy, and alone.

She rubs him roughly with a corner of her apron and carries him toward the house.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

THE BOY sits upright in bed, reaching toward NANA as she enters with the damp, mud-streaked VELVETEEN RABBIT.

(SOUND: rainwater hisses from the Rabbit's coat onto the hearth; the bedstead creaks)

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

NANA stops. THE BOY takes the Rabbit and clasps him beneath his chin.

(MUSIC: warm strings rise around the quiet nursery)

The VELVETEEN RABBIT'S dull boot-button eyes catch the firelight. A new wisdom and beauty settle into his shabby face.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The nursery magic had happened to him. He was a toy no longer. He was Real. The Boy himself had said it.

THE BOY falls asleep with both hands around him. The VELVETEEN RABBIT remains awake, almost overwhelmed by the love stirring inside his little sawdust heart.

EXT. WOOD GLADE - SUMMER EVENING

Beneath silver-green bracken, the much shabbier VELVETEEN RABBIT rests in a nest made by THE BOY. His whiskers are sparse, his ear linings gray, and his faded body has begun to lose its shape.

(SOUND: ants stir through dry grass; leaves whisper beneath a distant wood pigeon's call)

THE BOY, still round-faced beneath his chestnut fringe, runs away between the trees with a stick sword.

Two wild rabbits creep from the bracken. HAZEL is lean and russet with dark-tipped ears and powerful hind legs. CLOVER is compact, soft-furred, and brown all over. Both twitch their noses at the motionless toy.

HAZEL
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

HAZEL hops sideways and rises upon his hind legs.

HAZEL
Ho! It's as easy as anything. I don't believe you can!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He meant when the Boy threw him, but of course he didn't want to say so.

HAZEL
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The VELVETEEN RABBIT stiffens. The back of his stuffed body is made all in one piece, like a pincushion.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to!

HAZEL stretches his neck and peers beneath him.

HAZEL
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

HAZEL dances in a circle. CLOVER bounds after him.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

HAZEL
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

HAZEL comes close enough for his long whiskers to brush the Rabbit's sateen ear. He sniffs, flattens his ears, and recoils.

HAZEL
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Footsteps crash through the undergrowth. THE BOY runs past the glade.

(SOUND: HAZEL and CLOVER stamp the earth, leaves lash, and their fleeing paws vanish into silence)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

Only ants cross his velvet paws. The bracken sways where the wild rabbits disappeared.

(MUSIC: the playful woodland melody breaks into one lonely sustained note)

At sunset, THE BOY returns and carries the VELVETEEN RABBIT home. The Rabbit watches the empty bracken over THE BOY'S shoulder.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Weeks later, a fever lamp burns in the dark nursery. THE BOY'S chestnut hair is plastered to his flushed face, and his small body trembles beneath the bedclothes.

(SOUND: strained breathing, a medicine glass rattling, and the relentless tick of the clock)

The VELVETEEN RABBIT lies against him, his brown spots faded, his whiskers gone, and his gray-lined ears worn almost bare.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He scarcely looked like a rabbit any more, except to the Boy. To him he was always beautiful, and that was all the little Rabbit cared about.

NANA, her iron-gray bun above the same black dress and white apron, changes a cold cloth. DOCTOR LEWIS, a lean physician with silver side-whiskers and a black frock coat, examines THE BOY.

Whenever they approach, the VELVETEEN RABBIT burrows beneath the covers and lies perfectly still.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was afraid that if they found him someone might take him away, and he knew that the Boy needed him.

Night after night, the lamp burns. Medicine spoons empty. THE BOY tosses and murmurs while the Rabbit remains pressed against his burning chest.

(SOUND: wind worries the window frame; THE BOY'S breathing falters, then slowly steadies)

At last, sweat cools upon THE BOY'S face. His clenched hand relaxes around one of the Rabbit's shabby ears.

(MUSIC: the music-box theme returns, fragile but hopeful)

INT. NURSERY - MORNING

Bright sunlight pours through open windows. THE BOY, pale but alert beneath his chestnut fringe, sits wrapped in a blue shawl on the adjoining balcony.

(SOUND: a travel trunk clicks shut; curtains flap in the clean spring air)

Inside, the VELVETEEN RABBIT lies tangled among stripped bedclothes with only his worn head peeping out. NANA and DOCTOR LEWIS inspect the room.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Boy was going to the seaside tomorrow. Everything was arranged, and now it only remained to carry out the doctor's orders.

The Rabbit's dull eyes brighten at the sight of the travel trunk.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Hurrah! thought the little Rabbit. Tomorrow we shall go to the seaside!

NANA catches sight of him beneath the blanket.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR LEWIS
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once. What? Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

NANA seizes the Rabbit by his worn body. She drops him into a coarse sack with old picture-books and rubbish.

(SOUND: books thud into the sack; the drawstring scrapes tight)

The VELVETEEN RABBIT'S button eyes vanish beneath the closing cloth.

(MUSIC: the hopeful theme cuts off)

INT. GUEST BEDROOM - NIGHT

Moonlight crosses a freshly scrubbed bedroom. THE BOY, still pale and chestnut-haired, sleeps beneath clean sheets with a new white-plush rabbit beside him.

(SOUND: his calm breathing mingles with the distant rattle of carriage wheels being prepared)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night the Boy slept in a different bedroom, and he had a new bunny to sleep with him. But he was too excited to care very much, for tomorrow he was going to the seaside.

A stripe of moonlight ripples across the ceiling like a silver wave. THE BOY smiles in his sleep, unaware.

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

An untied sack lies among nettles and old boards behind the fowl-house. The VELVETEEN RABBIT wriggles his head through the opening as cold moonlight settles over his threadbare coat.

(SOUND: hens shift on their roosts; wind sighs through the raspberry canes)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The gardener had been too busy to make the bonfire. Next morning he promised to come quite early and burn the whole lot.

The Rabbit looks toward the raspberry thicket. In the moonlight, memories flicker across the canes: a green wheelbarrow, fairy huts, flower-bed burrows, and THE BOY'S arms reaching for him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

His boot-button eyes turn toward the distant nursery window. A real tear gathers, runs down his shabby velvet nose, and falls into the soil.

(MUSIC: every instrument fades, leaving a single trembling note)

From the place where the tear fell, emerald leaves push through the earth. A golden, cup-shaped blossom rises and opens.

(SOUND: roots softly crack the soil; a clear bell tone rings from the flower)

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY steps from the blossom. She is tiny and radiant, dressed in pearl and dewdrops, with flowers around her neck and woven through her golden hair.

She gathers the VELVETEEN RABBIT from the sack and kisses his damp nose.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

The Rabbit studies her flower-bright face.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to everyone.

The NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY holds him close and rises above the garden wall, carrying him toward the moonlit wood.

EXT. WOOD GLADE - MOONLIT NIGHT

Moonlight silvers the bracken and frosts the grass. Wild rabbits dance with their shadows between the tree trunks.

(SOUND: soft paws drum the turf; night insects sing beneath the rustling leaves)

The NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY descends with the VELVETEEN RABBIT. HAZEL, still lean and russet with dark-tipped ears, stops beside CLOVER, whose compact coat remains brown all over.

The other rabbits form a curious ring.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She kisses the VELVETEEN RABBIT and sets him on the grass.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

The VELVETEEN RABBIT sits motionless before the circle of strong hind legs.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He suddenly remembered about his hind legs, and he didn't want them to see that he was made all in one piece.

A blade of grass tickles his nose. Without thinking, he lifts a hind toe and scratches.

He freezes. The toe flexes.

(MUSIC: a celesta note blossoms into radiant strings)

The Rabbit's dingy velveteen becomes soft, shiny brown fur. Faint traces of his old spotted pattern remain beneath it. His ears twitch by themselves, long whiskers brush the grass, and living muscles gather beneath his body.

He leaps high over HAZEL, lands on four strong paws, and springs sideways. CLOVER races after him. The VELVETEEN RABBIT whirls and bounds among the others.

(SOUND: new paws thunder joyfully across the grass; leaves whip in their wake)

He stops and looks for the NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY, but only the golden flower glows where she stood.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOOD GLADE - SPRING DAY

Warm spring sunlight falls through new leaves. THE BOY, rosy and recovered, plays beneath the trees, his chestnut bowl-cut hair shining gold at the edges.

(SOUND: wood pigeons call, bees hum, and a brook chatters beyond the glade)

CLOVER, still brown all over, creeps from the bracken. Beside him comes the VELVETEEN RABBIT, now a sleek living rabbit with long whiskers, round black eyes, and the faint pattern of old spots beneath his brown fur.

THE BOY lowers his stick and stares.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And the Boy thought to himself, “Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!”

The VELVETEEN RABBIT holds THE BOY'S gaze. His nose twitches with something like remembrance.

(MUSIC: the nursery music-box theme returns in full, carried by warm strings)

CLOVER bounds into the bracken. The VELVETEEN RABBIT lingers for one final look, then leaps after him on his strong hind legs.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

The two rabbits race through spring grass and disappear together beneath the bright leaves.

(SOUND: their paws fade into birdsong)

FADE OUT.

> THE END <
